# `sum_over_time` over a mixed hist/float `or` subquery cross-branch
# collision (cerberus issues #3253, #3271, #3262).
#
# `sum_over_time` DROPS `__name__`, so the histogram series and the float
# series — distinct series, kept apart by the per-anchor `or` and folded
# separately — land on the SAME output label set `{x="1"}` once the fold
# recombines them. Upstream Prometheus, run the way the compat lane's real
# server actually runs (delayed-name-removal at its documented, currently
# shipping OFF default — see cerberus issue #3271 and
# test/spec/parityoracle/promql/oracle.go's EnableDelayedNameRemoval),
# refuses to build that vector at all: "vector cannot contain metrics with
# the same labelset". Cerberus's combineMixedFoldBranches
# (internal/promql/histogram_native_mixed_or_aggregate.go) now aborts the
# same way via VectorSetOp.MixedAbortOnCollision.
#
# This fixture used to pin a ONE-histogram-series answer, because at the
# time the spec oracle built its Prometheus engine with delayed name
# removal ON (promqltest.NewTestEngine's own hardcoded default), which
# silently merges the two name-collided series and then prefers the
# histogram by TYPE at materialisation. #3271's own measurement (against
# the real upstream engine, both settings) showed that outcome is neither
# `or`'s left bias — reversing the arms still answers the histogram — nor
# a range-stable answer — the SAME DNR=ON engine's range query returns one
# series carrying BOTH a float point and a histogram point at the
# identical timestamp, a matrix no emitter can reproduce byte-for-byte —
# it is an artefact of instant evaluation under the ON setting alone. Now
# that the spec oracle's EnableDelayedNameRemoval matches the real
# server's OFF default, re-running this exact seed through the reference
# engine (test/spec/parityoracle/promql's Evaluate) raises the identical
# "vector cannot contain metrics with the same labelset" cerberus's abort
# does — checked directly, not assumed.
#
# Seedless: the query must FAIL and the round-trip runner has no shape for
# an expected error — the same reason ceil_duplicate_labelset.txtar and
# label_join_duplicate_labelset.txtar are seedless. The executable half is
# in internal/api/prom/handler_chdb_mixed_or_subquery_fold_identity_test.go,
# which pins this exact rejection for all seven FOLD names (including
# sum_over_time) over both /api/v1/query and /api/v1/query_range, plus a
# distinct-labelset control that must still answer two series when the
# match key does not actually collide.
#
# subquery_last_over_time_mixed_or_partial_shadow.txtar is the sibling for
# `last_over_time`/`first_over_time`: #3271 measured that family answering
# TWO series under BOTH delayed-name-removal settings (it preserves
# `__name__`, so the two arms never land on one output label set), and it
# stays enrolled with seed/expected_rows/parity.
-- query.promql --
sum_over_time((latency_exp_hist or latency_float)[3m:1m])
-- parity_exempt --
reason: rejection-only
detail: This query must be REJECTED; it carries no seed/expected_rows because the round-trip runner has no shape for an expected error. The executable assertion lives in internal/api/prom/handler_chdb_mixed_or_subquery_fold_identity_test.go, not in a value comparison RunParity could perform.
